'''Wirral West''' is a [[county constituency|constituency]] represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] by [[Matthew Patrick (British politician)|Matthew Patrick]] of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] since [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024]].

==Constituency profile==
The constituency is one of four covering the [[Metropolitan Borough of Wirral]]. It includes [[Hoylake]], [[West Kirby]], [[Woodchurch, Merseyside|Woodchurch]] and the north edge of [[Heswall]]. This is one of the more affluent areas within [[Merseyside]],<ref>UK Polling Report http://ukpollingreport.co.uk/2015guide/wirralwest/</ref> reflected in higher house prices and wages than the wider North West region.<ref>Electoral Calculus https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/seatdetails.py?seat=Wirral+West</ref>

== History ==
Wirral West was created in [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983]] from the northern part of the former [[Wirral (UK Parliament constituency)|Wirral constituency]], which had traditionally elected [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] MPs. [[Selwyn Lloyd]] was the predecessor constituency's MP from 1945 to 1976, serving as [[Secretary of State for Foreign and Commonwealth Affairs|Foreign Secretary]] during the [[Eden ministry]], [[Chancellor of the Exchequer]] during the [[Conservative government, 1957-1964|Macmillan ministry]] and becoming [[Speaker of the House of Commons (United Kingdom)|Speaker of the Commons]] in 1971. Raised to the peerage in 1976, Lloyd resigned his seat and the ensuing [[1976 Wirral by-election|by-election]] was won by [[David Hunt, Baron Hunt of Wirral|David Hunt]], who became the first MP for Wirral West in 1983. Hunt was a Cabinet member under [[Margaret Thatcher]] and [[John Major]], serving as [[Secretary of State for Employment]] and twice as [[Secretary of State for Wales]].

Hunt held the seat until [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997]], when he lost to barrister [[Stephen Hesford]] of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]], but remained in Parliament as a member of the [[House of Lords]]. Hesford increased his majority in [[2001 United Kingdom general election|2001]] and narrowly retained the seat in [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005]], despite a challenge at the latter election from former TV presenter and Conservative [[Esther McVey]]. On 22 January 2010, he announced his intention to step down at the next general election for family reasons.

New boundary changes implemented, McVey stood again and won the seat for the Conservatives at the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 general election]], serving in the [[Cameron–Clegg coalition|coalition government]] as a [[Department for Work and Pensions|Work and Pensions]] minister during her tenure. If implemented in 2005, the 2010 boundary changes would have seen the Conservatives win the seat by 569 votes.

Wirral West had been described as a [[bellwether]] since its establishment,<ref>{{cite news|author=Joe Thomas|date=2015-04-22|title=General election 2015: 'Kingmaker' Wirral West voters hold keys to Downing Street|newspaper=[[Liverpool Echo]]|url=http://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/news/liverpool-news/general-election-2015-kingmaker-wirral-9093110|access-date=2015-05-12}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|author=Harry Lambert|date=2015-04-20|title=What are the top seats to watch in the election?|newspaper=[[New Statesman]]|url=http://www.newstatesman.com/politics/2015/04/what-are-top-seats-watch-election|access-date=2015-05-12}}</ref> but bucked the trend at the [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015 general election]] when Labour's [[Margaret Greenwood]] narrowly defeated McVey, despite the Conservatives winning a parliamentary majority. Like the nearby [[City of Chester (UK Parliament constituency)|City of Chester]], it was one of the few Conservative-held [[Marginal seat|marginals]] outside of London to be gained by Labour, who benefited from the constituency's collapse in [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrat]] support.

Sixth on the [[List of target seats in the 2017 United Kingdom general election|Conservative target list]] in [[2017 United Kingdom general election|2017]], Greenwood was comfortably re-elected to Wirral West with the highest winning vote share since the seat was first up for election in 1983. She was promoted to [[Shadow Cabinet of Jeremy Corbyn|Jeremy Corbyn's shadow cabinet]] as the [[Shadow Secretary of State for Work and Pensions|Shadow Work and Pensions Secretary]] in 2018, shadowing McVey who had returned to Parliament as the MP for [[Tatton (UK Parliament constituency)|Tatton]]. Greenwood was re-elected with a reduced vote share in [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019]], and departed from the shadow cabinet upon [[Keir Starmer]]'s election as Labour leader.

== Boundaries ==	
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Wirral West (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|frame-height=250|text=Map of current boundaries}}
'''1983–2010''': [[Metropolitan Borough of Wirral]] wards of Hoylake, [[Prenton (ward)|Prenton]], Royden, Thurstaston, and [[Upton (ward)|Upton]].

'''2010–present''': Metropolitan Borough of Wirral wards of [[Greasby, Frankby and Irby (ward)|Greasby, Frankby and Irby]], [[Hoylake and Meols (ward)|Hoylake and Meols]], [[Pensby and Thingwall (ward)|Pensby and Thingwall]], Upton, and [[West Kirby and Thurstaston (ward)|West Kirby and Thurstaston]].

In the 2005 Boundary Commission report, Wirral West lost part of the Prenton ward to the [[Birkenhead (UK Parliament constituency)|Birkenhead]] constituency, and gained part of [[Barnston, Merseyside|Barnston]] from [[Wirral South (UK Parliament constituency)|Wirral South]].

=== Proposed ===
Further to the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], enacted by the Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023, from the [[2024 United Kingdom general election]], the constituency will be composed of the following wards of the Metropolitan Borough of Wirral (as they existed on 1 December 2020):

* Clatterbridge; Greasby, Frankby and Irby; Heswall; Hoylake and Meols; Pensby and Thingwall; Upton (polling districts MC, MD and ME); West Kirby and Thurstaston.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 5 North West region}}</ref>

''To bring the electorate within the permitted range, the [[Clatterbridge]] and [[Heswall]] wards will be added from the, to be abolished, constituency of [[Wirral South (UK Parliament constituency)|Wirral South]]. The northern part of the [[Upton, Merseyside|Upton]] ward will be transferred to [[Wallasey (UK Parliament constituency)|Wallasey]].''

