'''Wirral South''' was a [[United Kingdom constituencies|constituency]]{{#tag:ref|A [[county constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} in [[Merseyside]] in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]]. It was represented from 1997 until 2024 by members of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]].{{#tag:ref|As with all constituencies, the constituency elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) by the [[first past the post]] system of election at least every five years.|group= n}}

Further to the completion of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], the seat was abolished and its area split between three other constituencies, to be first contested at the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=The 2023 Review of Parliamentary Constituency Boundaries in England – Volume one: Report – North West {{!}} Boundary Commission for England |url=https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/2023-review-volume-one-report/the-2023-review-of-parliamentary-constituency-boundaries-in-england-volume-one-report-north-west/ |access-date=2023-07-21 |website=boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk}}</ref>

==Constituency profile==
Wirral South covered the central part of the Wirral peninsula including most of Heswall, Bebington and Bromborough. Wages and house prices are higher than the averages for the North West. Electoral Calculus describes the demographic as "Centrist", reflecting average views on the left-right and liberal-conservative scales.<ref>Electoral Calculus https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/seatdetails.py?seat=Wirral+South</ref>

==History==
In 1983, Wirral South arose for election following the national boundary review by taking over parts of two seats that were abolished to create it: [[Wirral (UK Parliament constituency)|Wirral]] and [[Bebington and Ellesmere Port (UK Parliament constituency)|Bebington and Ellesmere Port]], held by the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] since 1923 and 1979 respectively.

;Political history
[[Barry Porter]] ([[Conservative Party (UK)|Con]]) won the seat the first time when it was fought and at the next two general elections. He had ousted the Labour party candidate from Bebington and Ellesmere Port in 1979 which he held until the election in 1983. Following the death of Porter in late 1996, a [[1997 Wirral South by-election|by-election was held in February 1997]], the last by-election of that Parliament, held a matter of weeks before the general election was called. It was won by [[Ben Chapman (politician)|Ben Chapman]] ([[Labour Party (UK)|Lab]]), who held the seat until retiring following controversy over his expenses.<ref>{{cite news|
url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2009/may/21/ben-chapman-expenses-stand-down|title=Ben Chapman becomes first Labour MP to stand down over expenses|date=21 May 2009|work=Chapman Resigns|publisher=The Guardian|access-date=2011-01-10|location=London}}</ref> Labour narrowly managed to hold on in the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 general election]], electing [[Alison McGovern]]. Since then it has consistently shifted towards Labour, she increased her majority to 4,599 in the 2015 election.<ref name="Guardian 2015 election">{{cite web|last1=Osborn|first1=Matt|last2=Straumann|first2=Ralph|last3=Franklin|first3=Will|last4=Clarke|first4=Seán|title=UK 2015 general election results in full|url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/ng-interactive/2015/may/07/live-uk-election-results-in-full|website=Guardian|publisher=Guardian Media News Group|access-date=5 May 2018}}</ref> She was re-elected in 2017 with a majority of 8,323, an increase of 7% over 2015, the biggest majority Labour has ever held in the seat and the biggest majority for any party in Wirral South since [[1987 United Kingdom general election|1987]].<ref name="BBC election 2017">{{cite news|title=Wirral South parliamentary constituency - Election 2017|work=BBC News|publisher=BBC|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/politics/constituencies/E14001043|access-date=5 May 2018}}</ref> In 2019 she won Re-Election by a slightly narrower 14% margin.

=== 2015 general election ===
The terms of the [[Fixed-term Parliaments Act 2011]] mandated that the election was held on 7 May 2015. [[Alison McGovern]] was the sitting [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] for the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]]. The [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] selected John Bell. Bell had previously stood for election in [[Clwyd South (UK Parliament constituency)|Clwyd South]] (2010)<ref>{{cite news|title=Election 2005: Result: Delyn|work=BBC News|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/shared/election2010/results/constituency/w22.stm}}</ref> and [[Delyn (UK Parliament constituency)|Delyn]] (2005).<ref>{{cite news|title=Election 2010: Result: Clwyd South|work=BBC News|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/shared/vote2005/html/180.stm}}</ref> He had also stood twice to be a [[Welsh Assembly Members|Welsh Assembly Member]].<ref>{{cite web|date=2011-05-05|title=Election results for Alyn and Deeside, 5 May 2011|url=http://www.senedd.assemblywales.org/mgElectionAreaResults.aspx?ID=4}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=BBC NEWS, Welsh Assembly Election 2007, Clwyd South|work=BBC News|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/shared/vote2007/welshasssembly_english/html/815.stm}}</ref> He stood in a local council by-election in 2011, for [[Wrexham County Borough Council|Wrexham County Council]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.wrexham.gov.uk/assets/pdfs/electoral/declaration_result_marchwiel_june11.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=2013-09-14 |archive-date=2013-12-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131213181653/http://www.wrexham.gov.uk/assets/pdfs/electoral/declaration_result_marchwiel_june11.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref>

== Boundaries ==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Wirral South (UK Parliament constituency)}}|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries 1983-2024}}
Since its creation in 1983, the constituency has consisted of the Metropolitan Borough of Wirral wards of Bebington, Bromborough, Clatterbridge, Eastham, and Heswall. The constituency is one of four covering the [[Metropolitan Borough of Wirral]] in [[Merseyside]].

=== Abolition ===
Further to the completion of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], the seat was abolished prior to the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]], with its contents distributed to three neighbouring constituencies:<ref name=":0" />

* [[Bromborough]] and [[Eastham, Merseyside|Eastham]] wards to the new seat of [[Ellesmere Port and Bromborough (UK Parliament constituency)|Ellesmere Port and Bromborough]]
* [[Clatterbridge]] and [[Heswall]] wards to [[Wirral West (UK Parliament constituency)|Wirral West]]
* [[Bebington]] ward to [[Birkenhead (UK Parliament constituency)|Birkenhead]]

